---
import Article from "~/components/Article.astro";
import BlockExplorer from "~/components/BlockExplorer.astro";
import Cite from "~/components/Cite.astro";
import ColoredFigure from "~/components/ColoredFigure.astro";
import ColoringKey from "~/components/ColoringKey.astro";
import Drawing from "~/components/Drawing.astro";
import EdgePuzzle from "~/components/EdgePuzzle.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Gallery from "~/components/Gallery.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Proof from "~/components/Proof.astro";
import References from "~/components/References.astro";
import Snark from "~/components/Snark.astro";
import Statement from "~/components/Statement.astro";
import Term from "~/components/Term.astro";
import { site } from "~/constants.ts";
import type { Graph } from "~/graphs/types.ts";

import { colorEdges } from "~/graphs/colorEdges.ts";
import { createBlockChain, stub } from "~/graphs/createBlockChain.ts";
import { createGoldbergGraph } from "~/graphs/createGoldbergGraph.ts";
import { createPetersenAroundBlock } from "~/graphs/createPetersenAroundBlock.ts";
import { createPetersenGraph } from "~/graphs/createPetersenGraph.ts";
import { getColorClass, getPartClass } from "~/graphs/edgeClasses.ts";
import BaseLayout from "~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const description =
	"What Goldberg snarks are, how they’re built, and a short proof of why three colors are never enough for their edges, with interactive diagrams.";

const sections = [
	{ id: "coloring", name: "Coloring edges" },
	{ id: "snarks", name: "What is a snark?" },
	{ id: "try", name: "Try to color one" },
	{ id: "building", name: "Building one" },
	{ id: "why-odd", name: "Why three colors fail" },
	{ id: "gallery", name: "Each snark" },
	{ id: "properties", name: "Properties" },
	{ id: "relatives", name: "Relatives" },
	{ id: "history", name: "History" },
	{ id: "references", name: "References" },
];

const block = createBlockChain(1);
const chain = createBlockChain(2);
const petersen = createPetersenGraph();
const ring = createGoldbergGraph(5);

// Its loose links are the same on both sides, so that it could be repeated
const loose = [...chain.edges.entries()].filter(([, { isLink }]) => isLink);
const chainColoring = colorEdges(chain, {
	fixed: new Map([
		[loose[0][0], 1],
		[loose[1][0], 0],
		[loose[2][0], 0],
		[loose[6][0], 1],
		[loose[7][0], 0],
		[loose[8][0], 0],
	]),
});

const cutPetersen = createPetersenAroundBlock();

/** Where names are drawn from vertices, so as to not cover any edges. */
const flatOffsets: Record<string, readonly [number, number]> = {
	a: [7, -5],
	b: [0, -7],
	c: [-8, 2],
	d: [8, 2],
	e: [0, -7],
	f: [0, -7],
	g: [-4, 12],
	h: [4, 12],
	u: [9, 3],
	v: [0, 14],
	w: [-10, 3],
};

const ringOffsets: Record<string, readonly [number, number]> = {
	a: [8, 7],
	b: [8, 3],
	c: [8, -2],
	d: [-5, -7],
	e: [0, -7],
	f: [-4, -7],
	g: [-9, 0],
	h: [10, 5],
};

const nameFlatVertex = (graph: Graph) => (index: number) => {
	const { role } = graph.vertices[index];

	return role === stub ? undefined : ([role, ...flatOffsets[role]] as const);
};

const citation = `@misc{goldbergsnarks,
  author = {Goldberg, Josh},
  title  = {Goldberg Snarks},
  year   = {2026},
  url    = {${site}}
}`;
---

<BaseLayout
	description={description}
	image={{
		alt: "The Goldberg snark G5, drawn as five blocks around a ring.",
		path: "/images/g5.png",
	}}
	schema={{
		"@type": "Article",
		about: { "@type": "Thing", name: "Goldberg snark" },
		headline: "Goldberg snarks",
	}}
	title="Goldberg snarks, explained"
>
	<Hero blocks={5}>
		<h1>Goldberg snarks</h1>
		<p>
			Link an odd number of identical pieces in a ring, and you get a graph
			whose edges can’t be colored with three colors. Nobody has to take that on
			faith: here’s what they are, how they’re built, and why it’s true.
		</p>
	</Hero>

	<Article sections={sections}>
		<section id="coloring">
			<h2>Coloring edges</h2>
			<p>
				A <em>graph</em> is a set of dots, called vertices, joined by lines,
				called edges. Where they’re drawn doesn’t matter: only what’s joined to
				what. The graphs on this site are all <em>cubic</em>, meaning every
				vertex has exactly three edges.
			</p>
			<p>
				To <em>color</em> a graph’s edges is to give each one a color such that
				edges meeting at a vertex are all different. A cubic graph needs at
				least three colors, because three edges meet at each vertex. A theorem
				of Vadim Vizing from 1964 says it never needs more than four.
			</p>
			<p>
				So every cubic graph either can be colored with three colors or just
				barely can’t. Most can. Of the cubic graphs on 28 vertices with no
				cycles shorter than four, fewer than one in a thousand need that fourth
				color
				<Cite id="brinkmann2026" />.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="snarks">
			<h2>What is a snark?</h2>
			<p>
				Snarks are the cubic graphs that need four colors. Some graphs need four
				for a dull reason, though, such as having been padded out from a smaller
				one. Those are ruled out.
			</p>
			<Statement kind="Definition" name="snark" id="snark">
				<p>
					A <em>snark</em> is a connected cubic graph whose edges can’t be
					colored with three colors, that has no cycle of fewer than five edges,
					and that can’t be separated into two parts that both have cycles by
					cutting fewer than four edges.
				</p>
			</Statement>
			<p>
				Authors differ on the last two conditions, and some leave them out. The
				smallest snark is the Petersen graph.
			</p>
			<ColoredFigure
				graph={petersen}
				label="The Petersen graph, a pentagon joined to a five-pointed star inside of it, with thirteen of its fifteen edges colored."
				size="small"
			>
				The Petersen graph, colored as well as it can be. Wherever three colors
				are put, at least two edges are <Term is="spare">left over</Term>.
			</ColoredFigure>
			<p>
				Snarks matter because they’re where hard problems hide. For several
				famous conjectures, such as the cycle double cover conjecture and the
				five-flow conjecture, it’s been proven that the smallest counterexample
				would have to be a snark
				<Cite id="brinkmann2026" />. Anyone hoping to find one looks among the
				snarks.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="try">
			<h2>Try to color one</h2>
			<p>
				This is <Snark n={5} />, the smallest Goldberg snark. Its 60 edges are
				yours to color. Vertices where two edges of a color meet are marked in{" "}
				<Term is="spare">pink</Term>.
			</p>
			<EdgePuzzle blocks={5} />
			<p>
				You can get to 58. The rest of this page is about why you can’t get to
				60, and why it’s no use trying for 59.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="building">
			<h2>Building a Goldberg snark</h2>
			<p>
				Every Goldberg snark is made of copies of one piece: a <em>block</em> of
				eight vertices, traditionally named <i>a</i> through <i>h</i>. Nine
				edges are within the block, and six loose links, three to a side, join
				it to its neighbors.
			</p>
			<Figure size="small">
				<Drawing
					slot="drawing"
					edgeClass={(index) => getPartClass(block, index)}
					graph={block}
					label="A block: eight vertices named a through h. Links cross it at three heights: through f and e at the top, g and h lower down, and a at the bottom. Between them, c joins b, e, and g; d joins b, f, and h; and b joins a."
					names={nameFlatVertex(block)}
					padding={12}
				/>
				A block and its six loose links: two each for the
				<Term is="outer">outer</Term>, <Term is="middle">middle</Term>, and
				<Term is="hub">hub</Term> rings.
			</Figure>
			<p>
				Blocks are cut from the Petersen graph. Take any three of its vertices
				in a row and cut them out: what remains is <i>b</i> through <i>h</i>,
				with five edges left dangling. The vertex <i>a</i> is then added below{" "}
				<i>b</i>.
			</p>
			<Figure size="medium">
				<Drawing
					slot="drawing"
					edgeClass={(index) =>
						cutPetersen.edges[index].isLink ? "cut" : "plain"
					}
					graph={cutPetersen}
					label="The Petersen graph, drawn as a block’s vertices b through h, with three more vertices: u, which joins e, h, and v; v, which joins b, u, and w; and w, which joins f, g, and v."
					names={nameFlatVertex(cutPetersen)}
					padding={14}
				/>
				The Petersen graph, drawn around a block. Cut out <i>u</i>, <i>v</i>,
				and <i>w</i>, and the dashed edges are left dangling.
			</Figure>
			<p>
				Link blocks side to side and the last back to the first. The links
				themselves join up into three rings: the{" "}
				<Term is="outer">outer ring</Term> through each block’s <i>e</i> and{" "}
				<i>f</i>, the <Term is="middle">middle ring</Term>
				through <i>g</i> and <i>h</i>, and the <Term is="hub">hub</Term> through{" "}
				<i>a</i>.
			</p>
			<Figure size="large">
				<Drawing
					slot="drawing"
					edgeClass={(index) => getPartClass(ring, index)}
					graph={ring}
					label="Five blocks in a ring. The outer and middle rings each pass through ten vertices, and the hub passes through five in the center."
					names={(index) => {
						const { block: number, role } = ring.vertices[index];

						return number === 0
							? ([`${role}1`, ...ringOffsets[role]] as const)
							: undefined;
					}}
					padding={10}
				/>
				Five blocks make <Snark n={5} />. The first block’s vertices are named.
			</Figure>
			<Statement kind="Definition" name="Goldberg snark" id="goldberg-snark">
				<p>
					For <i>k</i> ≥ 3, the <em>Goldberg graph</em>
					<Snark n="k" /> has 8<i>k</i> vertices{" "}
					<i>
						a<sub>i</sub>, b<sub>i</sub>, c<sub>i</sub>, d<sub>i</sub>, e
						<sub>i</sub>, f<sub>i</sub>, g<sub>i</sub>, h<sub>i</sub>
					</i>{" "}
					and 12<i>k</i> edges:
				</p>
				<p class="edges">
					<i>
						a<sub>i</sub>b<sub>i</sub>, b<sub>i</sub>c<sub>i</sub>, b
						<sub>i</sub>d<sub>i</sub>, c<sub>i</sub>e<sub>i</sub>, c<sub>i</sub>
						g<sub>i</sub>, d<sub>i</sub>f<sub>i</sub>, d<sub>i</sub>h
						<sub>i</sub>, e<sub>i</sub>f<sub>i</sub>, g<sub>i</sub>h<sub>i</sub>
					</i>
					<br />
					<i>
						a<sub>i</sub>a<sub>i+1</sub>, e<sub>i</sub>f<sub>i+1</sub>, h
						<sub>i</sub>g<sub>i+1</sub>
					</i>
				</p>
				<p>
					where <i>i</i> counts from 1 to <i>k</i>, and <i>k</i> + 1 wraps back
					to 1. When <i>k</i> is odd and at least 5, <Snark n="k" /> is a{" "}
					<em>Goldberg snark</em> <Cite id="mohar2006" />.
				</p>
			</Statement>
			<p>
				Whether <Snark n={3} /> counts depends on whom you ask.
				<a href="/g3">Its own page</a> has the arguments.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="why-odd">
			<h2>Why three colors fail</h2>
			<p>
				The trick is to ignore almost everything. Between two neighboring blocks
				there are three links. Look only at the <Term is="outer">outer</Term>{" "}
				and
				<Term is="middle">middle</Term> ones, and ask one question: are they the
				same color?
			</p>
			<Statement kind="Lemma" name="flipping" id="flipping">
				<p>
					However a block is colored with three colors, the outer and middle
					links match on exactly one of its two sides.
				</p>
			</Statement>
			<Figure>
				<Drawing
					slot="drawing"
					edgeClass={(index) => getColorClass(chainColoring?.[index])}
					graph={chain}
					label="Two blocks in a row, colored. On the left, the outer and middle links are both green. Between the blocks, they’re two different colors. On the right, they’re the same color again."
					padding={4}
				/>
				<ColoringKey />
				The upper two links match on the left, differ in the middle, and match
				again on the right. Each block flips them.
			</Figure>
			<Proof>
				<p>
					Set <i>a</i> aside. The other seven vertices have five dangling edges:
					the outer and middle links on the left, those on the right, and the
					edge down to
					<i>a</i>. Each color has an edge at each of the seven vertices. Edges
					inside account for two vertices at a time, and seven is odd, so each
					color is on an odd number of dangling edges. With five of them, one
					color is on three and the other two are on one each.
				</p>
				<p>
					<em>Both sides can’t match.</em> If they did, the four links would be
					two pairs. Then either two colors are on two edges each before
					counting the fifth, or one color is on four. Neither can become three,
					one, and one.
				</p>
				<p>
					<em>Both sides can’t differ.</em> If they did, the color that’s on
					three dangling edges must be once on the left, once on the right, and
					once on the edge to <i>a</i>. The other link on the left has a second
					color, and the other link on the right has the third. Now undo the
					cut: attach the right’s links to <i>u</i>, the left’s links to{" "}
					<i>w</i>, and the last edge to <i>v</i>. Color the edge from <i>u</i>{" "}
					to <i>v</i> with the second color, and the edge from <i>w</i>
					to <i>v</i> with the third. All three colors meet at each of <i>u</i>,{" "}
					<i>v</i>, and <i>w</i>, so the Petersen graph has been colored with
					three colors. That’s known to be impossible.
				</p>
			</Proof>
			<p class="aside">
				This site’s tests also check it the long way. Of the 729 ways to color a
				block’s six links, 72 can be extended to the rest of it, and all 72
				match on exactly one side.
			</p>
			<Statement kind="Theorem" name="parity" id="theorem">
				<p>
					The edges of <Snark n="k" /> can be colored with three colors if and
					only if
					<i>k</i> is even.
				</p>
			</Statement>
			<Proof>
				<p>
					Suppose that it’s colored, start between any two blocks, and walk once
					around the ring. Each of the <i>k</i> blocks flips the links from
					matching to differing or back. But you return to the very links you
					started at, so you must have flipped an even number of times. An odd{" "}
					<i>k</i> is impossible.
				</p>
				<p>
					When <i>k</i> is even, the pair of blocks above can be repeated, as
					its links can be colored the same on the right as on the left.
				</p>
			</Proof>
			<p>
				Change the number of blocks to see both halves of the theorem. The
				colors give out at every odd number, and recover at every even one.
			</p>
			<BlockExplorer />
			<h3>Why exactly two edges are left over</h3>
			<p>
				Coloring all but one edge would be a near miss, but there’s no such
				thing. Suppose only the edge between vertices <i>u</i> and <i>v</i> has
				no color. Every other vertex has an edge of each color, and <i>u</i> and{" "}
				<i>v</i> are each missing one color. Each color’s edges join vertices up
				in pairs, and there’s an even number of vertices, so an even number of
				them lack any given color. The color missing at <i>u</i> must then be
				the one missing at <i>v</i>. Give it to the edge between them and the
				whole graph is colored, which we know can’t be done.
			</p>
			<p>
				This works for any snark. Two is the best that could be hoped for, and
				every Goldberg snark drawn on this site achieves it.
			</p>
			<h3>No grand tour</h3>
			<Statement kind="Corollary" id="hamiltonian">
				<p>
					No Goldberg snark has a <em>Hamiltonian cycle</em>: a loop along its
					edges that visits every vertex exactly once.
				</p>
			</Statement>
			<Proof>
				<p>
					The loop would have an even number of edges, so two colors could
					alternate along it. It would use two of each vertex’s three edges, so
					the edges unused wouldn’t touch each other, and could all be given the
					third color.
				</p>
			</Proof>
			<p>
				They come as close as possible, though. Each one drawn here has a loop
				that misses only a single vertex.{" "}
				<a href="/g5">
					The page for <Snark n={5} />
				</a>{" "}
				shows one.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="gallery">
			<h2>Each snark</h2>
			<p>
				There’s a Goldberg snark for every odd number from five up. These are
				the first few, and their not-quite-a-snark sibling.
			</p>
			<Gallery />
		</section>

		<section id="properties">
			<h2>Properties</h2>
			<p>
				Anything that depends only on blocks grows steadily with the number of
				them, <i>k</i>. These hold for odd <i>k</i> ≥ 5.
			</p>
			<div class="table">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">Property</th>
							<th scope="col">Value</th>
							<th scope="col">How it’s known</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Vertices</th>
							<td>
								8<i>k</i>
							</td>
							<td>By definition</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Edges</th>
							<td>
								12<i>k</i>
							</td>
							<td>By definition</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Colors needed for edges</th>
							<td>4</td>
							<td>
								<a href="#theorem">Proven above</a>
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Colors needed for edges and vertices at once</th>
							<td>4</td>
							<td>
								Proven <Cite id="campos2011" />
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Shortest cycle (girth)</th>
							<td>5</td>
							<td>
								Computed for{" "}
								<span class="nowrap">
									<i>k</i> ≤ 15
								</span>
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Pentagons</th>
							<td>
								2<i>k</i>, or 11 for <i>k</i> = 5
							</td>
							<td>
								Computed for{" "}
								<span class="nowrap">
									<i>k</i> ≤ 15
								</span>
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Symmetries</th>
							<td>
								4<i>k</i>
							</td>
							<td>
								Computed for{" "}
								<span class="nowrap">
									<i>k</i> ≤ 15
								</span>
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Longest cycle</th>
							<td>
								8<i>k</i> − 1
							</td>
							<td>
								Computed for{" "}
								<span class="nowrap">
									<i>k</i> ≤ 15
								</span>
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">Fewest crossings in a drawing</th>
							<td>
								2<i>k</i>
							</td>
							<td>
								Reported for <i>k</i> ≤ 12, conjectured beyond{" "}
								<Cite id="weisstein" />
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				Crossings are never avoidable. Peter Tait showed in 1880 that the four
				color theorem, which says that four colors suffice for any flat map, is
				the same as saying that no planar graph is a snark{" "}
				<Cite id="tait1880" />. That theorem was proven in 1976, so every
				snark’s drawings have edges that cross.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="relatives">
			<h2>Relatives</h2>
			<dl>
				<dt>Twisted Goldberg snarks</dt>
				<dd>
					Cross over two of the links that join one pair of blocks, so that its{" "}
					<i>e</i> leads to the next one’s <i>g</i> and its <i>h</i> to the next
					one’s <i>f</i>. The flipping proof doesn’t notice, as it only asks
					whether those two links match <Cite id="campos2011" />.
				</dd>
				<dt>Loupekine snarks</dt>
				<dd>
					Snarks made from other snarks by the same cutting out of three
					vertices in a row. The Goldberg snarks are one way to arrange the
					pieces cut from Petersen graphs <Cite id="berman2017" />.
				</dd>
				<dt>Flower snarks</dt>
				<dd>
					An older family, also of odd rings of identical blocks, with four
					vertices in each <Cite id="isaacs1975" />.
				</dd>
				<dt>Even Goldberg graphs</dt>
				<dd>
					The same construction with an even number of blocks. Three colors are
					enough for them, so they’re not snarks.
				</dd>
			</dl>
		</section>

		<section id="history">
			<h2>History</h2>
			<p>
				For decades, snarks were almost never seen. Julius Petersen showed in
				1898 that his graph’s edges need four colors. Danilo Blanuša found the
				next two in 1946, and only two more had turned up by 1975, when Rufus
				Isaacs described the first infinite families of them{" "}
				<Cite id="isaacs1975" />.
			</p>
			<p>
				The name came a year later. Martin Gardner, writing in{" "}
				<cite>Scientific American</cite>, borrowed it from Lewis Carroll’s{" "}
				<cite>The Hunting of the Snark</cite>, for how elusive they’d been{" "}
				<Cite id="gardner1976" />.
			</p>
			<p>
				Mark K. Goldberg described the snarks on this site in 1981, in a paper
				on building cubic graphs that three colors aren’t enough for{" "}
				<Cite id="goldberg1981" />.
				<a href="https://www.cs.rpi.edu/~goldberg">Goldberg</a> earned a
				doctorate from the Institute of Mathematics in Novosibirsk and became a
				professor of computer science at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute. One
				of the main conjectures about edge colorings, the Goldberg–Seymour
				conjecture, also bears Goldberg’s name.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section id="references">
			<h2>References</h2>
			<References />
			<h3>Citing this site</h3>
			<pre tabindex="0"><code>{citation}</code></pre>
		</section>
	</Article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.nowrap {
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

